# Tutorial 03 — Expressions and Characters
# The full pipeline: @character, @charimg, @charlayer, @charpreset, [show], [hide].

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_03_expressions
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Right now I'm on screen with expression {b}talking{/b} at position {b}center{/b}.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Now it's {b}happy{/b}. Same position, different sprite. That swap is one line of script.
{{EXPRESSION_HINT}}

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> Step 1: @character — declaring a character -> section_declare
> Step 2: @charimg — simple sprites -> section_charimg
> Step 3: @charlayer + @charpreset — layered sprites -> section_layers
> Step 4: [show] and [hide] — putting characters on screen -> section_show_hide
> Step 5: Positions — where characters stand -> section_positions
> Quick reference: character pipeline -> quick_reference
> Live gallery: Lavender's expressions -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Character setup usually follows this order:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character lavender "Lavender"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender happy assets/characters/lavender_happy.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender eyes_happy assets/characters/lavender/eyes_happy.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charpreset lavender happy $base | $eyes_happy | $mouth_smile{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[hide lavender]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: declare once, use stable expression names, and keep positions readable before adding motion.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_declare
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Step 1: @character ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_declare
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Every character starts with a declaration:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character lavender "Lavender"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character alex "Alexander"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character narrator "Narrator"{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}lavender{/b} is the script ID — you'll type this in every [show] and dialogue line.
Lavender: {b}"Lavender"{/b} is the display name — what the player sees in the name box.
Lavender: The ID can be short ({b}lav{/b}) while the display name is full ({b}"Lavender Ashford"{/b}).
narrator: Declarations go in a shared file so every script can use them. For this project: {b}{{CHARACTERS_SCRIPT_PATH}}{/b}.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Declare once, use everywhere. Never duplicate @character lines across files.
> Next: @charimg -> section_charimg
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Step 2: @charimg ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_charimg
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}@charimg{/b} assigns a single image to an expression name:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender happy assets/characters/sprites/lavender_happy.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender sad assets/characters/sprites/lavender_sad.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender talking assets/characters/sprites/lavender_talking.png{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Structure: {b}@charimg{/b} {i}characterId{/i} {i}expressionName{/i} {i}imagePath{/i}
Lavender: One image per expression. When the engine shows "happy", it loads that exact file.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} Name files consistently: {b}charname_expression.png{/b} — lavender_happy.png, alex_angry.png.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Simple sprites are the fastest way to get a character on screen. One file = one face.
> Next: layered sprites -> section_layers
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Step 3: Layered Sprites ────────────────────────────────────

@label section_layers
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For more expressions with fewer art files, use {b}layered sprites{/b}. Two directives:
narrator: {b}@charlayer{/b} — registers an individual art layer (base body, eye variant, mouth variant):
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender base assets/characters/lavender/base.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender eyes_happy assets/characters/lavender/eyes_happy.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender eyes_sad assets/characters/lavender/eyes_sad.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender mouth_smile assets/characters/lavender/mouth_smile.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender mouth_frown assets/characters/lavender/mouth_frown.png{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
narrator: {b}@charpreset{/b} — combines layers into a named expression:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charpreset lavender happy $base | $eyes_happy | $mouth_smile{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charpreset lavender sad $base | $eyes_sad | $mouth_frown{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charpreset lavender bittersweet $base | $eyes_sad | $mouth_smile{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: See the power? 5 layers created 3 expressions. With 10 layers you could have 20+ combinations.
Lavender: Use @charimg for small casts. Use @charlayer + @charpreset when you need expression variety.
> Next: show and hide -> section_show_hide
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Step 4: [show] and [hide] ──────────────────────────────────

@label section_show_hide
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[show]{/b} places a character on screen. {b}[hide]{/b} removes them.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
narrator: Structure: [show] {i}characterId{/i} {i}position{/i} {i}expression{/i}
Lavender: Watch:
[hide lavender]
[wait 500]
narrator: She's gone. That was {b}[hide lavender]{/b}.
[wait 400]
[show lavender left happy]
Lavender: Back on the left with {b}happy{/b}. That was {b}[show lavender left happy]{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Change expression without moving — just keep the same position:
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
[show lavender center emphasis]
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center emphasis]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center talking]{/color}
Lavender: Each [show] is instant. The sprite swaps on the same frame — no animation.
> Next: positions -> section_positions
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Step 5: Positions ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_positions
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: This is {b}far_left{/b}.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}left{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}center{/b}.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}right{/b}.
[show lavender far_right talking]
Lavender: {b}far_right{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Five named positions. For precise placement, use normalized coordinates:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender at 0.70,0.84]{/color}
narrator: X = 0.70 means 70% from the left edge. Y = 0.84 means 84% from the top.
Lavender: You can also define reusable custom positions:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position doorway 0.85 0.82{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender doorway talking]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Named positions for most scenes. Custom coordinates when you need pixel precision.
> Expression gallery -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Expression Gallery ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let me cycle through my expressions so you can see each one. Watch the sprite change.
[wait 300]
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: {b}happy{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
[wait 600]
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: {b}talking{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center talking]{/color}
[wait 600]
[show lavender center emphasis]
narrator: {b}emphasis{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center emphasis]{/color}
[wait 600]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Now watch position and expression change together in a mini-scene:
[wait 300]
[hide lavender]
[wait 500]
narrator: The door swung open.
[show lavender far_right happy]
[wait 300]
Lavender: There you are!
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I've been looking everywhere.
[show lavender left emphasis]
Lavender: ...wait. What happened here?
[wait 400]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That was four [show] commands: far_right happy, center talking, left emphasis, center talking.
Lavender: Each one told the player where I was and how I felt — without a single narrator line explaining it.
Lavender: Open {b}{{CHARACTERS_SCRIPT_PATH}}{/b} to see how these expressions are declared, then try adding your own.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_03]
[flag tutorial_done_03]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
